00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.680
- Sure.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:00.330
So I'm Matt Fraser.

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.450
I actually started in
data science in 2008

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:06.600
right after the financial
crisis. Prior to that,

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.540
I was more of an insurance guy.
I actually have a background in philosophy,

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:14.160
specifically, metaphysics.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.850
And so, I'm sort of a
Carl Popperian at heart,

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.710
if anybody knows
who Karl Popper is,

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:22.770
who is the first
empirical skeptic. And so,

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:26.400
I sort of transitioned my

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.310
empirical skepticism over into
data science and started to

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:33.600
learn it in 2008. Before that,
I carried a number of executive

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.880
positions at different
insurance companies in the

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:36.840
underwriting space.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.280
At that point in time,
from let's say, 1998 through 2008,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.460
I did not know of an
insurance company that was actually doing

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:51.570
any predictive modeling at
all, and it is still the case.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:55.020
That, really, the National
carriers are using AI,

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.330
they're using data science,
they're using predictive

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.000
modeling. But most of
the smaller mutuals, right?

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.850
The ones that are only in
a couple of states are really

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:06.990
using other predictive modeling
software and service companies

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.110
to do all of their
predictive modelings. So

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.150
data science was a

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:15.690
sort of an obvious
transition after I made it.

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:19.180
But I had no idea what the
heck it was before I got into it.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:22.510
I was just a philosophy guy. I
was a language guy. I love language.

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.560
And I love understanding what is
knowable and what isn't knowable, right?

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:28.230
But I didn't really realize
that you could translate that

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.440
into mathematics
until I got into

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.630
a company called
Valen Technologies.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:38.040
So I sort of got out
of the insurance industry and went

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.770
into a company that
was adjacent to the insurance

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.080
industry. Actually, selling to

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:44.610
insurance companies

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:46.530
predictive analytics and

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:48.810
data science solutions.
So before that,

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:51.750
I was pretty good on
the actuarial side.

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:54.630
I'm not a classically
trained actuary at heart,

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.820
but I was the director
of pricing at a number of

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.650
different insurance companies

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:01.420
as I sort of progressed
through my career.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:04.770
And so I got pretty
good at using Excel.

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.900
I got pretty good at
grinding through data.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.080
I get pretty good at
philosophically understanding

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.660
data and understanding

00:04:12.660 --> 00:04:16.019
what it meant if certain
data had low integrity versus high

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:16.935
integrity and things like that.

00:04:16.935 --> 00:04:18.600
I was really good
at data wrangling.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:20.760
But I didn't have any
idea what a GLM was.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.120
And don't ask me to write
any formulas down because I let you

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:27.420
guys and I let the data
scientists do that, right?

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.600
But I'm pretty good at picking
models apart because of my

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:34.080
empirical skepticism that
I learned at a very early,

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.340
sort of, age through
college and high school.

00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:39.330
My dad was a physician. He
always used to hammer me on,

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.580
hey, are you sure you know
that? Do you know that?

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:42.990
Or do you think you
know that, right?

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.810
Do you believe you know
that because beliefs can be wrong?

00:04:46.810 --> 00:04:48.390
And so, when I was at Valen,

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:52.740
I was able to get trained
by a former chief modeling officer

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:57.870
at Capital One. Valen Technologies,
one of the major investors

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:01.290
was Nigel Morris, who is one
of the co-founders of Capital One.

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:06.480
And Capital One was sort of one
of the first banks that got into

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.360
machine learning, got
into predictive modeling

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:11.580
on the credit
card side. And so,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.830
they had a very
robust understanding.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:15.570
There were one of the
early adopters let's say,

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:18.660
in the banking space and
the fintech space of actually using

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.840
predictive models in order
to create a competitive advantage

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.770
or arbitrage opportunity within the
credit card space. And so,

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:29.490
we were able to really
capitalize on that connection

00:05:29.490 --> 00:05:30.570
with Capital One.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:33.780
We're able to take some
really smart people from Capital One,

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:38.130
bring them into Valen. And
I really learned on the job

00:05:38.130 --> 00:05:39.960
from one of the former

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.700
chief modeling officers on
the commercial auto and auto

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:47.460
finance side from Capital One.

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:48.780
And

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:51.690
the second I got into it,
I absolutely loved it. I mean,

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:54.660
Valen was my favorite job. The
second I got into data science,

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:55.410
I was like, Oh, God,

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.360
this is what I've been
missing my whole life, right?

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.440
Like, I love the language,

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.380
but to be able to blend the
language and the math together,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:02.970
the language and
the data together,

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:07.020
and actually produce
something that gives another

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:10.740
company a moat or a
competitive advantage in a marketplace--

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:12.390
man, that's really cool. Like,

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:16.260
I can actually take things
from the philosophy side where all

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.620
you're really doing is creating
more questions for yourself and

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:23.550
actually translating that into real
durable value in the world.

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:25.950
Like something that's tangible
that can be seen through

00:06:25.950 --> 00:06:28.770
dollars and cents, which
is super duper excited.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.030
I got really
excited about that.

00:06:31.030 --> 00:06:33.780
And then, I
translated that into

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:34.410
Pie Insurance.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:38.130
I was one of what I would
call the silent co-founder

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:43.230
at Pie. We took all of the
learnings that we were able to

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:45.390
extract from Valen
Technologies,

00:06:45.390 --> 00:06:48.120
working with about 56 different
insurance companies in the

00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:49.530
property and casualty space.

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.920
And we translated that
into building durable models that

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:55.860
created a competitive advantage
and an arbitrage opportunity

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.340
for Pie Insurance Pie
started in-- oh, gosh,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:02.550
I want to say it was 2018

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:06.510
now. So we're now writing
about 300 million.

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:10.330
We're on the path to about
350, maybe, $400 million this year.

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:13.260
And I believe we're
the fastest growing

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.810
and quickest,

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:19.230
most capitalized InsurTech

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.660
that has ever
existed. So, somehow,

00:07:21.660 --> 00:07:23.820
I was able to take that
philosophy degree translate it

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:27.030
into data science, create
a competitive advantage, and

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:30.870
raise a whole bunch of money
and create a company that now employs.

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:31.560
Unfortunately,

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:34.110
we just went through what
we call a reduction force.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.820
But we had about 460

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:39.180
employees. We now have
about 400 employees now.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:42.210
And I think that's the end
of the cutting, so far.

00:07:42.210 --> 00:07:43.260
But

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.200
I've been very pleased with what
I've been able to do and

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:48.420
what I've been able to
learn in data science.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:50.250
And I'm just looking forward to

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:53.150
a lot more learning
opportunities as we move forward.

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:55.670
Now, you asked what
do I like to do?

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:57.070
- Yeah.

00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:01.000
- Skiing, fly fishing,
hiking. I like to be outside.

00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.010
So as long as it's outside,

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:06.080
and it's sort of more
of a single sport,

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:07.420
I really like that
kind of a thing.

