that I'm not big on
teams because I'm huge on teams,

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:13.960
I just don't play a
lot of team sports.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:19.300
I play tennis and things like that
are more double or single kind of thing.

00:08:19.300 --> 00:08:23.432
- Well, where are you outside
right now in your background? Your--

00:08:23.432 --> 00:08:24.580
virtual background.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:29.890
- This is Wadi Rum, so
this is right near Petra in Jordan.

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:33.710
And I try to keep it fresh
with all my background.

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:36.100
So I have goofy backgrounds
like Animal House and

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:38.120
Casablanca and other
things like that.

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:40.780
This one just happened to
be up on my Zoom today.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:45.070
- Well, thank you so much for
joining us today, Matt. I feel like--

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:47.950
I can feel the energy
from you and excitement coming through

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:52.570
the computer. So thank you
for bringing the energy here today.

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:54.520
Something I

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:57.130
really wanted to ask
you about, initially,

00:08:57.130 --> 00:09:00.070
because this is something that comes
up quite a bit in the

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:01.330
Data Science Hangouts.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:05.890
So I know you wrote your
bio for the hangout with ChatGPT,

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:09.700
so I feel like this is a
good place to start off.

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:13.930
But I'd love to hear
your thoughts on chat-- ChatGPT,

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:15.610
but also AI in general.

00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:19.700
And how you think it may
impact the insurance industry.

00:09:19.700 --> 00:09:22.180
- OK. I'll start
with in general,

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:24.280
and then we'll go
into insurance.

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:26.320
There's

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:29.410
a lot of people on
both sides of one particular argument,

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.570
and that is whether ChatGPT

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:35.200
has the latest sort of tangible
instantiation of AI, right?

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:36.850
Is AI going to take
over the world?

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:40.600
Are we going to live in a
terminator universe where AI

00:09:40.600 --> 00:09:42.820
eventually is significantly smarter
than us and somehow

00:09:42.820 --> 00:09:45.160
grows in tension?

00:09:45.160 --> 00:09:48.490
I'm in the camp that
I think that artificial intelligence

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:52.480
and large language models
and the like are

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.350
going to be a
benefit to society.

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:56.680
They are not going to be
a threat to society. Now,

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:58.750
do we have to be careful?
Of course, right?

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:03.640
Human beings are never at
a space morally where we are

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:08.560
technologically. And so, the technology
is going to force us

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:13.060
to reevaluate or trans evaluate
are scale of values. We're

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.710
going to have to understand
what can be done,

00:10:15.710 --> 00:10:18.790
and we're going to have to
understand how we can make sure

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:23.170
that we are distributing things
like AI and some equitable manner.

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:24.310
And

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:27.080
in insurance, this is
a real big problem,

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:29.260
especially in the
industry that I'm in.

00:10:29.260 --> 00:10:32.350
We have a lot of small
carriers out there,

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:34.160
but they don't have
any of the data.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:38.980
There are about five or six
or seven very large carriers that beat

00:10:38.980 --> 00:10:42.530
all of the small
carriers up with data.

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:44.440
Now, we're not talking
about large language models,

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:46.540
we're literally just
talking about simple GLMs.

00:10:46.540 --> 00:10:48.850
We're talking about any of
the normal machine learning

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:50.590
algorithms, whether they
be gradient boosting,

00:10:50.590 --> 00:10:52.270
whether they be random forest.

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:54.910
Whether they be other

00:10:54.910 --> 00:10:58.360
elastic nets or tree models,
it doesn't really matter.

00:10:58.360 --> 00:10:59.860
Whoever has the most data wins.

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:02.170
Usually in insurance,
right? Into insurance,

00:11:02.170 --> 00:11:04.160
it's a little bit weird.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.420
We have a zero point
inflation on all of the distributions

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:08.390
that we're looking at.

00:11:08.390 --> 00:11:11.590
So, in most cases, when
you look at an insurance policy,

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:16.180
about 75% to 97% of the time,
the right answer is zero,

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:17.860
right? People just
don't have losses.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:19.300
But when they have losses,

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:20.920
some of them can be
very significant.

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:23.620
And they can really hurt
businesses. They can hurt individuals.

00:11:23.620 --> 00:11:25.540
And that's what
insurance is for.

00:11:25.540 --> 00:11:29.320
Insurance is for that highly
unlikely event that is

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:30.740
catastrophic, right?

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.890
That's where insurance
really helps society.

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:34.000
And at the end of the day,

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.340
that's a really interesting problem
to solve for a lot of

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:38.110
data scientists, right?

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.130
The issue is,

00:11:39.130 --> 00:11:42.190
that most of the carriers that
are out there that aren't the

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:45.070
big national guys, don't have
enough data in order to do

00:11:45.070 --> 00:11:49.360
that, right? And that's
a big problem because you

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.520
can use that to your advantage
to become a monopoly.

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:53.290
And if you see Hartford,
if you see travelers,

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:54.520
if you see Liberty Mutual,

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.860
if you see Swiss Re or some
of the large reinsurers,

00:11:56.860 --> 00:11:59.250
they have all the data.

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:02.790
So they can almost always build
better models because, at this point,

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:06.150
the algorithm doesn't matter as much
as how much data you have

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:07.990
and the integrity of that data.

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:11.220
It's really important to
understand your problem space

00:12:11.220 --> 00:12:13.890
and to think very deeply
about that problem space where you

00:12:13.890 --> 00:12:16.920
can go get data in order to
solve that problem in space. And so,

00:12:16.920 --> 00:12:18.990
at Valen Analytics,
when I was there,

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:20.940
we actually created
a consortium data set,

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:23.310
and that was specifically
for small carriers.

00:12:23.310 --> 00:12:26.820
It was to allow small carriers
and new entrants to the

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:30.450
insurance market to be able
to take advantage of large data

00:12:30.450 --> 00:12:33.600
sets with people who had
subject matter expertise and

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:37.350
data science expertise to be
able to deploy models for those

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:40.620
small carriers and democratize
the insurance space,

00:12:40.620 --> 00:12:44.100
to really allow new entrants
and innovation into that space.

00:12:44.100 --> 00:12:46.500
If anybody knows anything
about the insurance space now,

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:49.530
the one thing that is
very, very clear is that,

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:53.190
it is probably one of
the slowest-moving industries on the planet.

00:12:53.190 --> 00:12:55.770
And the reason that it's
one of the slowest-moving industries

00:12:55.770 --> 00:12:56.910
on the planet is because,

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.770
there are five carriers
that hold all the data,

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:00.420
and they don't have to move.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:03.250
They already have a
durable advantage over everybody else.

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:05.550
And so, they're not
really interested in innovating.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:05.880
Now,

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:08.670
that's changed in the last
decade quite a bit because of

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:09.840
people like Valen,

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:13.350
and other software as a
service companies that are coming out,

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:15.720
or data sciences service
companies that are building

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:19.470
consortium data sets. Or
finding novel ways to use

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:23.040
public source data in
order to create large,

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:25.890
either empirical or
synthetic data sets,

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:29.190
that can bring value to bear
for some of these smaller

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:32.310
carriers, and allow
new entrants into

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:34.650
the industry. And what I
think that's going to do is--

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:36.570
first of all, it's going
to disrupt the industry.

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:38.140
That's why

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:40.440
the insurance industry has
coined a new term called

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:42.660
Insurtech, right?

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:45.330
Most of those InsurTech
companies are not just tech.

00:13:45.330 --> 00:13:48.750
They should call it like
insure data or insure machine learning

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.330
or insure AI because that's
really what all of the

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:53.370
Insurtechs are doing,
they're using AI,

00:13:53.370 --> 00:13:55.590
they're using machine
learning. It's really not AI,

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:57.600
it's machine learning, right?

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:59.940
It's building models on large

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:02.280
consortium or
synthetic data sets

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:06.300
in order to extract or
provide that value to smaller carriers

00:14:06.300 --> 00:14:07.680
so they can compete
with the big guys.

00:14:07.680 --> 00:14:11.880
Because most of the small
guys, either just, sort of, larger

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:16.140
along, like, barely soldier on
through the snow in Siberia, right?

00:14:16.140 --> 00:14:20.020
Or they just get subsumed or bought
or acquired by some of the large guys.

00:14:20.020 --> 00:14:22.590
And so, they just get
bigger, and they continue their monopoly.

00:14:22.590 --> 00:14:25.170
What I'm really interested
in is making sure that data

00:14:25.170 --> 00:14:29.730
science and large available
data sets can actually

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:32.580
democratize the industry that I'm
in because I believe it's

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:33.390
an unfair industry.

00:14:33.390 --> 00:14:38.220
And I believe it's a quasi
cabal of monopolists that are

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:39.570
actually controlling
this industry.

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:42.670
And that is true in
many, many industries.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:45.160
And I think there's going
to be a huge opportunity,

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:47.580
especially with the
advent of ChatGPT

00:14:47.580 --> 00:14:52.590
and these LLMs for software as
a service companies to go out

00:14:52.590 --> 00:14:56.970
and very easily get customers
in order to optimize those

00:14:56.970 --> 00:14:58.620
standardized LLMs,

00:14:58.620 --> 00:15:02.400
and do other data science
and machine learning projects for

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:05.310
these companies, deploy
those models for them,

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:09.630
and allow them to hit those models
with APIs and bring that value to bear.

00:15:09.630 --> 00:15:10.050
Now,

00:15:10.050 --> 00:15:13.260
there's a lot more insurance
companies that are looking to create

00:15:13.260 --> 00:15:16.260
data science practices
inside of their own carriers.

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:19.650
The biggest issue is you have
to get a critical mass of data

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:20.820
in order to do that. I mean,

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:24.160
Pie is writing $350
million in premium a year,

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:25.470
and we don't have enough data.

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:28.590
We actually had to go to
Valen to purchase additional

00:15:28.590 --> 00:15:31.770
consortium data in order to
build a robust model, right?

00:15:31.770 --> 00:15:33.270
Because of that 0-point mass.

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:36.540
So you have to be-- not
just a slightly big insurance company.

00:15:36.540 --> 00:15:39.420
You have to be about a six
$700 million insurance company

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.330
that's been writing for three to
five years at that level in

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:44.920
order to have enough data
to build a really robust model.

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:48.090
And so that's where I
think using consortium data sets or

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:52.650
novel data sets or even some
of these new algorithms, like,

00:15:52.650 --> 00:15:54.210
adversarial

00:15:54.210 --> 00:15:58.440
algorithms to create synthetic data
sets that are very closely

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:01.470
mirrored to the
underlying empirical data,

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:04.440
to be able to bootstrap and
expand that data to be able to

00:16:04.440 --> 00:16:06.450
make more robust models.

00:16:06.450 --> 00:16:08.490
There's a lot of companies that
I've talked to that are coming

00:16:08.490 --> 00:16:10.050
out with things like that.

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:13.080
And Pie is actively doing a
lot of POCs with those types of

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:14.190
companies to find out.

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:16.650
Whether we can take our
smaller data set and we can

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:19.410
exponentiate that data set so that
we have a much larger data

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:21.740
set to be able to
build models on.

00:16:21.740 --> 00:16:25.340
- Really interesting. Thank you.
I see Liz had a question.

00:16:25.340 --> 00:16:27.530
A follow-up question
in the chat. Liz,

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:29.530
do you want to jump in here?

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:32.280
- Sure.

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:36.660
So I worked in the Casualty
Space as an analyst for five

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:38.370
years. And I'm actually

00:16:38.370 --> 00:16:41.850
about to have an interview
next week with four junior data

00:16:41.850 --> 00:16:45.300
scientists trying to
get back in the space.

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:47.400
But I was curious
regarding ChatGPT.

00:16:49.410 --> 00:16:51.660
I think there's a lot
of people doing, like,

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:54.000
parrot programming
and stuff with that.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:57.570
And they maybe don't realize
that when they submit a

00:16:57.570 --> 00:17:00.930
question or a
prompt to ChatGPT,

00:17:00.930 --> 00:17:06.420
if it's not a locally
hosted LLM that the host is

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:07.690
owning that data,

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:11.760
whether they're allowed to or not
and that creates a huge exposure.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:15.569
I'm curious if you have any
thoughts or comments about that

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:17.369
liability and that exposure.

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:19.800
- Yeah. That's actually
a massive liability.

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:22.138
And I don't think that
the insurance space is

00:17:25.490 --> 00:17:27.810
going to capitalize on ChatGPT,

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:33.020
especially in the pricing
and underwriting domain until

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:36.800
there is assurances that the data
that they put in to train

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:39.530
specific instances of the LLM

00:17:39.530 --> 00:17:43.640
are going to be protected
in some way and cordoned off

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:45.630
from the larger language model.

00:17:45.630 --> 00:17:47.820
So, right now,

00:17:47.820 --> 00:17:50.930
I know that there are several companies
that I'm working with are like, well,

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:53.540
we don't want to do anything
until we have absolute

00:17:53.540 --> 00:17:57.770
assurance that our data is
safe and that no one else can

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:02.830
gain any advantage through the use
of us putting that data into,

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:03.980
right? Some can--

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:07.670
some container or some space that
allows us to train those LLMs.

00:18:07.670 --> 00:18:12.230
And I think that is where a
lot of new companies are going to

00:18:12.230 --> 00:18:14.930
be extremely successful
is going to be

00:18:14.930 --> 00:18:20.630
prompt engineering and specialization
of those larger language models,

00:18:20.630 --> 00:18:22.070
sort of, the base models.

00:18:22.070 --> 00:18:25.190
And making sure that they're doing
that in such a way that

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:27.140
they can make sure that
they have an understanding of the

00:18:27.140 --> 00:18:28.400
custody of the data.

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:31.850
And that they can either
tokenize that data or make sure

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:35.540
that data doesn't get into
the larger pool of data that's

00:18:35.540 --> 00:18:39.680
being used in order to
optimize the underlying base LLM. It's

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:40.730
really important.

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:42.470
I think until that happens,

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:46.460
ChatGPT is going to
be used more for--

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:50.960
in insurance, it's going to
be used for automating processes

00:18:50.960 --> 00:18:54.650
around changing policies, right?
Making sure that the language

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:57.680
in the chat functionality goes
back and forth very smoothly

00:18:57.680 --> 00:19:01.610
without any human intervention.
But I don't think that

00:19:01.610 --> 00:19:02.645
it's really going to the--

00:19:02.645 --> 00:19:05.510
the insurance industry is really
going to capitalize on ChatGPT

00:19:05.510 --> 00:19:07.970
as an underwriting
agent, right?

00:19:07.970 --> 00:19:12.500
Until all of that
data can is safe

00:19:12.500 --> 00:19:15.350
and is completely separated
and mutually exclusive from the

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:16.700
larger pool of data.

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:18.830
That's going to be a
really important piece of the puzzle

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:20.660
that some of this software,
as a service companies,

00:19:20.660 --> 00:19:23.470
are going to have
to figure out.

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:28.810
I mean, I know that I've
already told my team at Pie, and

00:19:28.810 --> 00:19:31.540
I work with several other
carriers, and I've told them, oh,

00:19:31.540 --> 00:19:34.120
don't put your data into
chat right now because then

00:19:34.120 --> 00:19:38.380
everybody gets to use it,
right? Like, this thing learns. And--

00:19:38.380 --> 00:19:42.970
- It's like, it's also about
work product and stuff, you know?

00:19:42.970 --> 00:19:43.830
- Oh, totally.

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:45.130
- If

00:19:45.130 --> 00:19:46.010
you say, oh, hey,

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:49.600
I need help fixing this chain
ladder model or whatever,

00:19:49.600 --> 00:19:51.760
but you have a little bit
of secret sauce in there,

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:54.310
and then someone else asks,
and then, boom, your secret's out,

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:55.660
and you lose an edge.

00:19:55.660 --> 00:19:58.930
- That's exactly right. I
mean, even in our business,

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:01.540
we have a very
specialized transformation of the target

00:20:01.540 --> 00:20:04.270
space that we use for our
line of business worker's

00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:07.390
compensation. And we don't
want anybody to know that.

00:20:07.390 --> 00:20:09.400
That's a specialized
transformation.

00:20:09.400 --> 00:20:11.410
I haven't seen anybody
else in the industry used.

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:13.240
It works really, really well.

00:20:13.240 --> 00:20:16.630
I don't want to give that
information to anybody, right?

00:20:16.630 --> 00:20:18.280
And the second I
pop it in there,

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:22.390
it's possible for
someone to extract that.

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:26.290
Unless new technology and

00:20:26.290 --> 00:20:29.440
updated knowledge is actually
given to the insurance industry

00:20:29.440 --> 00:20:32.620
by some of, you know,
a new company, a new individual,

00:20:32.620 --> 00:20:34.360
that's working with
these companies that actually

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:38.560
understands how to specialize
these LLMs without delivering

00:20:38.560 --> 00:20:41.900
the data to the
larger baseline model.

00:20:41.900 --> 00:20:43.590
I personally don't know
how to do that yet.

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:45.740
But I know that that's
something that's being talked

00:20:45.740 --> 00:20:49.160
about very actively and heavily
within the insurance space.

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:50.460
Well, how do we take
advantage of this?

00:20:50.460 --> 00:20:51.980
And if we're going to
take advantage of this,

00:20:51.980 --> 00:20:55.220
how do we not give
away our secret sauce

00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:57.600
and take advantage of
it, at the same time?

00:20:57.600 --> 00:20:59.460
- Oh, like, from
my perspective,

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:03.320
it seems like the best
use would be like having a

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:05.810
privately hosted

00:21:05.810 --> 00:21:07.280
LLM that serves as,

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:10.610
like a communication agent
for people filing claims or

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:12.350
whatever in their
apps, you know?

00:21:12.350 --> 00:21:15.470
Where it's kind of low stakes.
You can segment the data,

00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:19.100
make sure it doesn't get
out, but you also have clear

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:22.670
knowledgeable communication happening
that's automated and

00:21:22.670 --> 00:21:23.780
understandable.

00:21:23.780 --> 00:21:26.750
And the higher-end
stuff, like, maybe,

00:21:26.750 --> 00:21:30.890
we'll get there, but that's
like pretty not there right now.

00:21:30.890 --> 00:21:32.340
- I think that's exactly right.

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:34.790
The other big piece of the
puzzle that is going to be

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:40.850
there is, right now, almost
all insurance companies are--

00:21:40.850 --> 00:21:44.870
all insurance companies that
are using machine learning, are

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:48.560
using it in order to
straight through process some accounts.

00:21:48.560 --> 00:21:51.590
But there's always a human
being overlay, right?

00:21:51.590 --> 00:21:55.310
So you allow the model to
make about 30% to 50% of your

00:21:55.310 --> 00:21:58.710
decisions. And then, the other 50%
of the decisions, 50 to, let's say,

00:21:58.710 --> 00:22:02.360
30% of the decisions are
sent to an individual underwriter in

00:22:02.360 --> 00:22:05.540
the world in order to
make a human, right?

00:22:05.540 --> 00:22:10.520
With the advent of LLMs, a lot
of that need to have a human

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:13.340
individual in the
mix goes away, right?

00:22:13.340 --> 00:22:15.660
Or gets pushed
back in the funnel.

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:19.820
So you can allow a
machine learning model,

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:21.980
which is highly

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:25.190
calibrated and constrained,
right? And then you--

00:22:25.190 --> 00:22:26.090
here's the trick,

00:22:26.090 --> 00:22:29.690
you have to blend that together
with an LLM that is highly

00:22:29.690 --> 00:22:33.470
specified and constrained and
optimized that those two pieces

00:22:33.470 --> 00:22:35.870
of the puzzle work
together, right? And then,

00:22:35.870 --> 00:22:39.110
you can remove the underwriter
and use them after the decision

00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:41.960
is made as a check, right?

00:22:41.960 --> 00:22:45.260
So you've got a human in
the loop who is essentially making

00:22:45.260 --> 00:22:47.270
sure that the decision

00:22:47.270 --> 00:22:49.220
didn't have any
gaps in it, right?

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.580
Or a human that can identify
the gaps that exist in the

00:22:52.580 --> 00:22:54.900
system so that you can improve
the system. Eventually,

00:22:54.900 --> 00:22:56.510
what you get is that, maybe,

00:22:56.510 --> 00:23:00.020
you've got a human that needs
to be involved 5% of the time

00:23:00.020 --> 00:23:03.020
instead of a human that needs
to be involved 50% of the time.

00:23:03.020 --> 00:23:06.200
And the decisions are
more consistent and create more

00:23:06.200 --> 00:23:09.870
separation between the good risk
and the bad risk, right?

00:23:09.870 --> 00:23:12.540
Now, the other big piece
of that puzzle is equitability.

00:23:12.540 --> 00:23:17.370
So are you treating everyone
the same across the parameters

00:23:17.370 --> 00:23:19.680
that matter, right?
Whether it's demographic,

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:21.540
whether it's race, whether
it's something else,

00:23:21.540 --> 00:23:23.730
you've got to make sure that
you've got a human in there

00:23:23.730 --> 00:23:27.660
that is making sure that
the AI, the LLM, and

00:23:27.660 --> 00:23:31.260
the GLM or the machine
learning models

00:23:31.260 --> 00:23:33.030
are actually not

00:23:33.030 --> 00:23:36.360
introducing some specific bias
into the overall solution.

00:23:36.360 --> 00:23:36.435
-

00:23:36.435 --> 00:23:40.230
That was actually one of
the anonymous questions was how do

00:23:40.230 --> 00:23:45.150
you ensure that bias is
not introduced when generating synthetic data?

00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:46.310
- So--

00:23:46.310 --> 00:23:48.210
it's an awesome question,

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:50.630
and it depends on what
you mean by bias, right?

00:23:50.630 --> 00:23:52.140
Because there's bias,

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:54.560
whether it's statistical
bias or some other bias,

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:57.440
inherent in every single
model that's built, right?

00:23:57.440 --> 00:23:59.840
What we try and do

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:03.770
is we look very closely at
the variables that are going into

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:04.700
all of our models,

00:24:04.700 --> 00:24:07.190
and that's what's really
hard to do with an LLM.

00:24:07.190 --> 00:24:08.030
I mean, you know,

00:24:08.030 --> 00:24:11.030
somebody else almost has to
make sure that there's no bias

00:24:11.030 --> 00:24:13.670
in the baseline LLM.

00:24:13.670 --> 00:24:15.320
But it's all about
the covariates.

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:17.250
So when we're building
a machine learning model,

00:24:17.250 --> 00:24:19.100
we're taking a look
at the covariates,

00:24:19.100 --> 00:24:21.470
and we're making sure
that those covariates are highly

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:26.240
empirical and that they're
based on insurance data

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:27.140
for the most part.

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:29.600
Now, there's a lot of
demographic data that can come

00:24:29.600 --> 00:24:32.070
in, especially in the line
of business that I'm in,

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:34.100
which is worker's compensation.

00:24:34.100 --> 00:24:37.310
If anybody doesn't know what
worker's compensation is,

00:24:37.310 --> 00:24:39.410
it's essentially
a coverage that

00:24:39.410 --> 00:24:43.820
employers buy so that
their employees are covered

00:24:43.820 --> 00:24:45.350
if they have an accident.

00:24:45.350 --> 00:24:48.050
And it pays for all of
the medical bills, plus

00:24:48.050 --> 00:24:51.590
their weekly wage as long as
they cannot get back to work at

00:24:51.590 --> 00:24:53.840
the same world, right?

00:24:53.840 --> 00:24:55.650
And when you think about that,

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:57.170
there's a lot of
demographics that come in,

00:24:57.170 --> 00:25:00.920
things like average household
size, are actually important.

00:25:00.920 --> 00:25:03.320
Oddly, average hail
size is as important.

00:25:03.320 --> 00:25:07.100
And you can't imagine what
kind of biases that actually introduces.

00:25:07.100 --> 00:25:09.140
So if you're a
homeowner's insurer,

00:25:09.140 --> 00:25:10.820
average hail size
really matters, right?

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:12.980
Like the people in
Colorado, right?

00:25:12.980 --> 00:25:16.430
They have higher premiums
than the people in

00:25:16.430 --> 00:25:19.010
North Dakota because North
Dakota has less hail than

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:20.150
Colorado does, right?

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:22.490
Washington has less
hail than Colorado does,

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:24.350
whereas Washington has
different problems, right?

00:25:24.350 --> 00:25:27.200
They have floods and things
like that. California

00:25:27.200 --> 00:25:29.040
is highly biased
because of other things.

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:35.030
But what you really care about
is, is there some group of people

00:25:35.030 --> 00:25:36.620
that already have it tough?

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:38.840
And are we making it more tough
because of something that's

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:41.750
inherent in a covariate
that we're using

00:25:41.750 --> 00:25:43.670
that just makes it more
difficult for those people to

00:25:43.670 --> 00:25:45.320
purchase worker's
compensation insurance?

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:48.390
We try as best as we can
to remove those variables,

00:25:48.390 --> 00:25:50.840
whether it's average
wage, average household,

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:55.190
any race demographics, anything
like that, is completely just

00:25:55.190 --> 00:25:57.270
dumped out of our data set
right at the beginning,

00:25:57.270 --> 00:26:01.010
even though those exist, right?

00:26:01.010 --> 00:26:05.580
And so, we do our best to
try and minimize those biases. But, again,

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:09.500
there's always going to
be inherent and potentially unknown

00:26:09.500 --> 00:26:13.850
or unknowable biases that
actually exist within an overall

00:26:13.850 --> 00:26:17.750
algorithm estimation. So
we do the best that we can.

00:26:17.750 --> 00:26:20.480
Nobody's perfect at this.

00:26:20.480 --> 00:26:22.670
There's a lot of new companies
that are coming out with novel

00:26:22.670 --> 00:26:24.240
data like behavioral data.

00:26:24.240 --> 00:26:26.790
And that's something that the
industry is talking about very,

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:28.580
very closely, right?
It's like, hey,

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:33.680
if we can capture what
your media content consumption is, right?

00:26:33.680 --> 00:26:36.770
And that has a significant risk
separation between the good

00:26:36.770 --> 00:26:38.720
risk and the bad risk.

00:26:38.720 --> 00:26:42.680
Is it highly correlated with
things like race? Is it highly

00:26:42.680 --> 00:26:43.670
correlated with other things?

00:26:43.670 --> 00:26:47.090
Because it may be the case
that people have different

00:26:47.090 --> 00:26:49.880
backgrounds, consume
different media.

00:26:49.880 --> 00:26:54.140
And that may inherently have
some sort of correlation to

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:58.520
things that kind of matter
with regard to the current

00:26:58.520 --> 00:27:03.830
zeitgeist, in terms of which groups are

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:06.960
having trouble and which
groups aren't, right?

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:10.400
And so, there's a lot
of conversation there.

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:11.070
- Definitely.

00:27:11.070 --> 00:27:13.970
I see a lot of great questions
coming into the chat,

00:27:13.970 --> 00:27:16.970
so I want to make sure I
get to some of these too.

00:27:16.970 --> 00:27:18.150
- I'll be more concise.

00:27:18.150 --> 00:27:20.970
- No, no, no. These are
the great discussion.

00:27:20.970 --> 00:27:24.470
I just want to make sure I
get some of these too. But-- but,

00:27:24.470 --> 00:27:27.590
and it's I see you had asked
a question a bit earlier in the

00:27:27.590 --> 00:27:30.930
chat. Do you want
to jump in here?

00:27:30.930 --> 00:27:32.870
- I think you largely
answered it, Matt.

00:27:32.870 --> 00:27:35.240
But I was just really
interested when I went shopping

00:27:35.240 --> 00:27:38.090
for life insurance recently
that there were some companies

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:41.450
that were not the biggest
companies that were able to do

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:44.270
everything instantly online
and didn't require things like

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:48.110
blood draws or physicals or any
of that kind of physical evidence.

00:27:48.110 --> 00:27:51.410
And I was assuming that
was because they are using their

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:54.280
models. Or is it
just because they

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:56.950
are big enough that they
can kind of marginalize and don't

00:27:56.950 --> 00:27:58.000
need to be that specific?

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:00.790
I'm just curious what sets up
one company to be able to do

00:28:00.790 --> 00:28:04.030
that in one not to
be able to do that.

00:28:04.030 --> 00:28:06.760
- There's a lot of
moving pieces there. So, generally

00:28:06.760 --> 00:28:08.230
speaking, what I would say is,

00:28:08.230 --> 00:28:10.330
those people who
are doing it online,

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:13.150
their overall rate level is
a little bit higher, right?

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:14.980
Because they don't have all
of the data that some of the

00:28:14.980 --> 00:28:17.750
larger people who are not
doing it online actually have.

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:21.580
And so, let's say, an extremely
healthy individual, OK?

00:28:21.580 --> 00:28:25.750
You have no medical biomarkers.
You have gone to some company

00:28:25.750 --> 00:28:29.320
and gotten a very
extensive blood draw done,

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:32.230
and you have a really good
understanding of how healthy you are, right?

00:28:32.230 --> 00:28:36.280
You're very likely going to
get a better rate with a large

00:28:36.280 --> 00:28:39.070
company, who's not online, than
a smaller company, who's online.

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:40.420
Having said that,

00:28:40.420 --> 00:28:45.190
smaller companies are using more novel
data than the older companies are,

00:28:45.190 --> 00:28:49.450
in order to be able
to allow consumers to purchase those

00:28:49.450 --> 00:28:51.040
types of products online.

00:28:51.040 --> 00:28:53.860
And the rate level isn't
that different, right?

00:28:53.860 --> 00:28:57.100
You are going to get a discount
if you get your blood drawn and

00:28:57.100 --> 00:29:00.680
do the whole thing, if you
have extremely good biomarkers,

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:03.100
right? But if you
don't have extremely good biomarkers,

00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:05.440
it's probably the
best choice for you

00:29:05.440 --> 00:29:08.110
to go online and just get
the policy because they're going to

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:09.220
look at completely
different stuff.

00:29:09.220 --> 00:29:09.520
Now,

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:11.530
you have to understand that
a lot of those life insurance

00:29:11.530 --> 00:29:13.530
companies are also grabbing

00:29:15.040 --> 00:29:18.850
a lot of that data that many
of us don't even know they're

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:22.660
grabbing, right?
So-- so purchasing

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:24.430
patterns,

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:28.240
media content consumption
trends, behavioral trends,

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:30.340
these kinds of things that
they can actually grab.

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:32.560
So they're using your digital
fingerprint a heck of a lot

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:36.070
more than they're using
your biomarkers, right?

00:29:36.070 --> 00:29:37.540
And so, you're going
to get a higher rate,

00:29:37.540 --> 00:29:40.690
but they can still do a
really good job of separating risk,

00:29:40.690 --> 00:29:43.360
finding out whether you're
really interested in drinking a

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:46.780
lot of Jack Daniel's or whether
you're really interested in

00:29:46.780 --> 00:29:49.840
drinking a lot of mushroom tea.

00:29:49.840 --> 00:29:51.670
So those are going to
be very, very different.

00:29:51.670 --> 00:29:53.890
And companies now

00:29:53.890 --> 00:29:56.980
can use a lot of
these third-party

00:29:56.980 --> 00:30:00.190
API aggregators in order to
get all of that information and

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:02.980
consume that information
through deep learning models

00:30:02.980 --> 00:30:07.150
that are actually quite good
at separating good life insurance

00:30:07.150 --> 00:30:09.150
risks from bad life
insurance risks.

00:30:11.326 --> 00:30:11.760
- Thank you.

00:30:11.760 --> 00:30:13.760
- Did that answer
your question?

00:30:15.850 --> 00:30:18.190
- I see a thumbs-up.

00:30:18.190 --> 00:30:19.270
So

00:30:19.270 --> 00:30:21.460
Arafat had asked the
question. He put the star,

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:23.890
so I'll read it. But
it's-- the question was,

00:30:23.890 --> 00:30:26.870
love seeing the energy
in your communication.

00:30:26.870 --> 00:30:31.130
Do you have any tips on
how we can improve our communication?

00:30:31.130 --> 00:30:32.230
- So,

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:36.760
I have been talking to large groups
of people for a very long time.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:39.910
When I started, I

00:30:39.910 --> 00:30:41.080
pictured everybody
in their underwear.

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:44.320
I'm sure you've heard that,
it's an old trope, right?

00:30:44.320 --> 00:30:45.280
But

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:48.760
I think one of the most
important things to understand

00:30:48.760 --> 00:30:50.050
about people, in general,

00:30:50.050 --> 00:30:54.220
is we all put our underwear
on the same way. We

00:30:54.220 --> 00:30:57.040
all take a number two and a
number one in exactly the same way.

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:02.380
I-- very early in my career,

00:31:02.380 --> 00:31:05.590
was able to essentially
come right out of college,

00:31:05.590 --> 00:31:09.070
and I got an underwriting
position at an insurance company

00:31:09.070 --> 00:31:12.430
that was very small. I was
employee number five, and so,

00:31:12.430 --> 00:31:15.910
I had direct access to the CEO since

00:31:15.910 --> 00:31:17.090
I was right out of college.

00:31:17.090 --> 00:31:20.260
So my working environment
did not include any social

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:22.660
hierarchy like
everything was flat.

00:31:22.660 --> 00:31:26.530
And so that's what I would
encourage all of you to do is,

00:31:26.530 --> 00:31:28.280
if you're really going
to speak your truth,

00:31:28.280 --> 00:31:33.040
you have to be able
to punch through

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:34.940
the

00:31:34.940 --> 00:31:37.070
imposter syndrome
that you might have,

00:31:37.070 --> 00:31:40.990
the impression of a social
hierarchy that you might have.

00:31:40.990 --> 00:31:43.850
You have to be able to talk to
a board member or a CEO in the

00:31:43.850 --> 00:31:47.060
same way that you would talk
to a colleague because we're all

00:31:47.060 --> 00:31:49.100
the same, we're all humans.

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:51.980
We all have the same needs, and

00:31:51.980 --> 00:31:53.420
maybe

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:56.100
some people are smarter than
others, and that's fine.

00:31:56.100 --> 00:31:58.070
But if you really want to do
a good job of communicating,

00:31:58.070 --> 00:32:01.610
you have to communicate on
the same level as another individual,

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:04.430
which means you have to assume
that you are on the same level

00:32:04.430 --> 00:32:05.810
as the other individual.

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:08.300
I would just encourage
everybody to understand that

00:32:08.300 --> 00:32:11.810
we're all human beings.
We all have our foibles.

00:32:11.810 --> 00:32:14.060
There's something that's
running around on LinkedIn

00:32:14.060 --> 00:32:17.000
right now, and it is you

00:32:17.000 --> 00:32:19.460
have to make sure that you are

00:32:19.460 --> 00:32:22.070
really open and that you're
listening to other human

00:32:22.070 --> 00:32:24.830
beings, not just what they're
saying but how they're saying

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:29.570
it. And the important reason
for that is that in anyone's

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:33.770
life, any other human being only
knows about 1% to 2% of

00:32:33.770 --> 00:32:35.770
what's going on in their life.

00:32:37.580 --> 00:32:38.000
And so,

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:41.390
I would just really encourage
you to empathize and consider

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:45.590
yourself on exactly the same level
as the people that you're talking to.

00:32:45.590 --> 00:32:48.560
And it doesn't matter whether
they're a manager or senior

00:32:48.560 --> 00:32:51.530
manager, a director, a
VP, or a C-level employee,

00:32:51.530 --> 00:32:55.700
or even a board member or an
investor if you talk to them as

00:32:55.700 --> 00:32:58.790
if you are on their same
level and they're on your same level,

00:32:58.790 --> 00:33:01.520
you will get better
responses. I promise you,

00:33:01.520 --> 00:33:03.120
you'll get better responses.

00:33:03.120 --> 00:33:05.180
And I know it's easy to say,

00:33:05.180 --> 00:33:09.410
and it's a lot harder to
do because that anxiety comes up.

00:33:09.410 --> 00:33:11.720
If that anxiety comes up,
picture them in their underwear.

00:33:11.720 --> 00:33:14.330
Understand that they take a crap
just like the rest of us,

00:33:14.330 --> 00:33:16.370
right? And they've got
their own problems.

00:33:16.370 --> 00:33:20.240
You know, I got where I
got because I was lucky.

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:22.140
I made a couple of
the right choices,

00:33:22.140 --> 00:33:25.550
and I think probably 90% of
the people that are actually in

00:33:25.550 --> 00:33:29.210
positions like mine, they
got there through dedication,

00:33:29.210 --> 00:33:31.070
they got there through

00:33:31.070 --> 00:33:33.980
making sure that they were
very consistent at a couple of

00:33:33.980 --> 00:33:37.490
things that made sure that
they were person that could be

00:33:37.490 --> 00:33:39.950
trusted, and that they were
a person that could be counted

00:33:39.950 --> 00:33:45.050
on, right? So doing those small
things every single day is

00:33:45.050 --> 00:33:46.250
going to do wonders for you.

00:33:46.250 --> 00:33:48.830
Now, it's great if
you come up with a

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:50.180
Svengali move,

00:33:50.180 --> 00:33:52.190
and you come up with the
greatest thing that the company

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:55.340
has ever seen, right? That's
sort of an added bonus.

00:33:55.340 --> 00:33:58.460
But where you win is doing
small consistent things every single day,

00:33:58.460 --> 00:34:02.840
and talking to everyone like they're
a brother or like their sister,

00:34:02.840 --> 00:34:06.050
and making sure that you
are really empathizing with them

00:34:06.050 --> 00:34:09.980
and that they know that you
have the company's interests at

00:34:09.980 --> 00:34:12.350
heart, that you have
their interests at heart,

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:14.510
and that you're listening
to what their needs are.

00:34:14.510 --> 00:34:15.960
If you can do that,

00:34:15.960 --> 00:34:18.650
then you will become much more
confident in how you speak

00:34:18.650 --> 00:34:19.920
very, very quickly.

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:22.130
Now, I will say, I've got
a lot of bombast you--

00:34:22.130 --> 00:34:24.290
my wife tells me,
again and again,

00:34:24.290 --> 00:34:26.870
that I'm the only person she's
ever met that doesn't give a

00:34:26.870 --> 00:34:28.760
crap what anybody
else says about them.

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:30.290
And that's completely true.

00:34:30.290 --> 00:34:32.929
I just don't worry about
my image that much.

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:35.300
I worry about
being a good human.

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:37.880
I don't worry about being
a pretty human or professional

00:34:37.880 --> 00:34:39.739
human or the smartest
human on the planet.

00:34:39.739 --> 00:34:43.130
I just worry about being
the good human that people can

00:34:43.130 --> 00:34:46.310
trust and that people can
count on, and that's it.

00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:48.495
-

00:34:48.495 --> 00:34:51.389
What you just said about
doing those small consistent things

00:34:51.389 --> 00:34:56.460
was reminding me of that video I
saw you just posted on LinkedIn about

00:34:56.460 --> 00:35:00.330
being an effective leader
isn't just like going to the

00:35:00.330 --> 00:35:04.920
leadership workshop one time a
year and saying, OK, well, I'm

00:35:04.920 --> 00:35:07.750
trained on being a leader.

00:35:07.750 --> 00:35:08.850
And so,

00:35:08.850 --> 00:35:11.790
I was also wondering,
like, what are other

00:35:11.790 --> 00:35:15.150
things that you think
make someone an effective leader?

00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:19.000
- So,

00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:21.310
there's a couple of things
that I posted on LinkedIn.

00:35:21.310 --> 00:35:24.340
Some of them are quite old.
There was one that I posted

00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:26.140
that-- and I'm sure
everybody's seen this,

00:35:26.140 --> 00:35:30.250
the difference between a
boss and a leader, right?

00:35:30.250 --> 00:35:35.290
A boss sits behind you with a
whip and yells at you to get

00:35:35.290 --> 00:35:39.190
stuff done. And a leader is
the one who's pulling you along.

00:35:39.190 --> 00:35:43.030
He's or she is the one who is

00:35:43.030 --> 00:35:46.660
removing the blocks, who is
actually serving the employees.

00:35:46.660 --> 00:35:48.730
And I consider myself,

00:35:48.730 --> 00:35:53.020
and I'm a strong believer
in servant leadership, right? I'm

00:35:53.020 --> 00:35:54.580
not anybody's boss.

00:35:54.580 --> 00:35:57.970
I am the person who is
responsible for making sure

00:35:57.970 --> 00:36:00.340
that all of the
conditions are set up,

00:36:00.340 --> 00:36:06.040
so that each and every one of
my employees can succeed and can fully

00:36:06.040 --> 00:36:07.120
explore

00:36:07.120 --> 00:36:09.880
the domain of
their competencies.

00:36:09.880 --> 00:36:12.040
That's my job, right?

00:36:12.040 --> 00:36:15.760
My job is to find smart
people, and then set up the conditions,

00:36:15.760 --> 00:36:17.180
and get the heck
out of the way.

00:36:17.180 --> 00:36:19.390
And if there's a blocker
that is in their way,

00:36:19.390 --> 00:36:23.470
I need to go find a way
to remove that blocker,

00:36:23.470 --> 00:36:25.570
right? Now, are there
performance reviews and things

00:36:25.570 --> 00:36:27.550
like that? Yeah, every
company has them.

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:29.810
I am a strong
proponent against them.

00:36:29.810 --> 00:36:31.510
So I hate performance reviews.

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:33.580
I think that performance
reviews should be done every

00:36:33.580 --> 00:36:36.010
single day between the
individuals that are working

00:36:36.010 --> 00:36:39.640
together. And I don't think it
should be between a boss and

00:36:39.640 --> 00:36:40.900
that boss's employees,

00:36:40.900 --> 00:36:43.090
I think it should be
the individuals on--

00:36:43.090 --> 00:36:46.150
that their colleagues on the
team that are working together

00:36:46.150 --> 00:36:47.020
with one another.

00:36:47.020 --> 00:36:48.910
That's where that feedback
should be coming from.

00:36:48.910 --> 00:36:52.330
It should be a 360-degree
feedback because performance

00:36:52.330 --> 00:36:55.570
reviews are a thing that
can be weaponized so easily.

00:36:55.570 --> 00:36:57.670
And they say more about
the person who's giving the review

00:36:57.670 --> 00:37:00.580
than they do about the
person who's being reviewed, period.

00:37:00.580 --> 00:37:01.660
That is my position.

00:37:01.660 --> 00:37:02.110
Now,

00:37:02.110 --> 00:37:05.920
I'm happy to argue with
anyone about that particular position

00:37:05.920 --> 00:37:08.890
because of how strongly I
actually believe in that position.

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:12.010
I think that if we
can create an egalitarian society where

00:37:12.010 --> 00:37:14.680
everyone is responsible to
everyone else and everyone is

00:37:14.680 --> 00:37:19.360
giving constant feedback to everyone else
on how to improve with empathy

00:37:19.360 --> 00:37:20.980
and with honesty,

00:37:20.980 --> 00:37:24.430
that is where you can create
an organization or a social

00:37:24.430 --> 00:37:27.820
structure and a social
hierarchy that actually allows

00:37:27.820 --> 00:37:30.760
everyone to win, and
allows everyone to succeed,

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:35.230
and allows everyone to explore all
of the levels of Maslow's hierarchy.

00:37:35.230 --> 00:37:39.390
That's how you become
fully human and fully yourself.

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:40.950
- Thank you, Matt.

00:37:40.950 --> 00:37:43.980
I promise I will get to
some of the other insurance questions,

00:37:43.980 --> 00:37:47.340
but I want to be selfish for
just a second here and ask a

00:37:47.340 --> 00:37:51.420
question. When you said part
of the role in a leader is

00:37:51.420 --> 00:37:55.000
removing some of those
blockers for your team.

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:59.040
And I'm wondering for some of us
here who maybe are in a role

00:37:59.040 --> 00:38:03.240
where we've identified
certain blockers for us,

00:38:03.240 --> 00:38:06.500
how would you
recommend you go about

00:38:06.500 --> 00:38:09.800
sharing that with your
leadership to kind of get past

00:38:09.800 --> 00:38:12.310
those roadblocks?

00:38:12.310 --> 00:38:15.400
- Whether it's an I or
a we, right? Sometimes,

00:38:15.400 --> 00:38:17.290
there's a blocker for a
whole team. And sometimes,

00:38:17.290 --> 00:38:19.960
there's a blocker for
an individual, right?

00:38:19.960 --> 00:38:22.240
I think what you
need to do is--

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:25.030
I don't know if any of
you have taken any courses in nonviolent

00:38:25.030 --> 00:38:27.160
communication, it's
kind of a big thing now.

00:38:27.160 --> 00:38:29.800
We've taken a lot in
nonviolent communication.

00:38:29.800 --> 00:38:30.760
I don't believe all of it,

00:38:30.760 --> 00:38:33.640
but I think it is a toolset
where you can sort of mix and

00:38:33.640 --> 00:38:35.920
match some of the tools
that come out of nonviolent communication.

00:38:35.920 --> 00:38:40.090
One of those things is to
listen for needs and to express

00:38:40.090 --> 00:38:43.120
needs, right? So
you start with,

00:38:43.120 --> 00:38:45.610
I'm trying to do
this for the company.

00:38:45.610 --> 00:38:49.690
I believe that this is going to
be very valuable, do you agree? Right?

00:38:49.690 --> 00:38:50.410
So, first,

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:53.260
you've got to get buy-in on
what you're trying to do is

00:38:53.260 --> 00:38:56.570
valuable for the organization
and valuable for the company.

00:38:56.570 --> 00:38:59.600
The second thing is,
in order to do that,

00:38:59.600 --> 00:39:02.390
I have certain needs
that are not being met.

00:39:02.390 --> 00:39:04.600
This is what those needs are--
it could be an individual,

00:39:04.600 --> 00:39:06.430
could be a group of
individuals, it could be a

00:39:06.430 --> 00:39:09.730
dynamic, it could be
technology that isn't there,

00:39:09.730 --> 00:39:12.460
it could be budget, it could
be whatever, right? And then,

00:39:12.460 --> 00:39:15.430
you really express
that as a need,

00:39:15.430 --> 00:39:18.460
not as a so-and-so is doing
something to me, and I'm mad, right?

00:39:18.460 --> 00:39:21.580
That's a very-- sort of
a selfish communication.

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:24.610
What you want to do is you
want to try to abstract that as much

00:39:24.610 --> 00:39:25.750
as you can. Now,

00:39:25.750 --> 00:39:28.120
you can't always because
sometimes there's just a person

00:39:28.120 --> 00:39:30.050
who isn't on board, right?

00:39:30.050 --> 00:39:31.040
And so, you say, look,

00:39:31.040 --> 00:39:33.730
I really need this
person to be on board,

00:39:33.730 --> 00:39:36.050
but I don't think this
person is on board.

00:39:36.050 --> 00:39:42.010
And I want to ask you what we
can do together in order to try and

00:39:42.010 --> 00:39:47.500
identify that other person's needs
and discover why it is

00:39:47.500 --> 00:39:50.020
that they appear to be a
blocker. It may be in my own

00:39:50.020 --> 00:39:52.270
head, and I'm just-- I'm

00:39:52.270 --> 00:39:53.770
just in an echo chamber.

00:39:53.770 --> 00:39:55.850
But let's have
that conversation.

00:39:55.850 --> 00:39:58.810
And if you don't feel
confident having that conversation yourself,

00:39:58.810 --> 00:40:01.330
go to someone who you think
can have that conversation.

00:40:01.330 --> 00:40:02.470
And it doesn't have
to be a manager,

00:40:02.470 --> 00:40:06.070
it could be a colleague
who knows that person really well,

00:40:06.070 --> 00:40:08.830
and can set up that meeting
so that you can have that meeting

00:40:08.830 --> 00:40:10.750
and identify what
those needs are.

00:40:10.750 --> 00:40:12.730
There's another book that
I would really strongly

00:40:12.730 --> 00:40:15.850
recommend, and unfortunately, it's
escaping me right now.

00:40:15.850 --> 00:40:18.010
The guy's name is Chris Voss,
it's called Never

00:40:18.010 --> 00:40:18.370
Split The

00:40:18.370 --> 00:40:20.110
Difference, I don't know if
any of you have read that.

00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:23.110
I strongly encourage
you to read that book.

00:40:23.110 --> 00:40:24.610
Some of it's throwaway, right?

00:40:24.610 --> 00:40:26.470
But you know I follow
the Pareto principle,

00:40:26.470 --> 00:40:32.110
so there's always 20% of
absolutely fantastic nuggets in every book.

00:40:32.110 --> 00:40:36.961
So that book basically
teaches you to get to know.

00:40:36.961 --> 00:40:40.300
You talk to an individual, and
you have a dialogue with them.

00:40:40.300 --> 00:40:41.770
You have a dialectic with them,

00:40:41.770 --> 00:40:44.710
until you get to why they're
saying no to whatever thing you

00:40:44.710 --> 00:40:46.300
want to get through, right?

00:40:46.300 --> 00:40:49.970
Because yeses that aren't
really yeses, don't matter.

00:40:49.970 --> 00:40:51.610
You have to get
to the no, right?

00:40:51.610 --> 00:40:53.020
And so, that--

00:40:53.020 --> 00:40:55.030
I think making sure that you
understand what the other

00:40:55.030 --> 00:40:56.890
person's needs are.

00:40:56.890 --> 00:41:00.100
Or the organization's
needs, if that's a blocker,

00:41:00.100 --> 00:41:03.220
and then making sure that
you're constructing the right conversation,

00:41:03.220 --> 00:41:05.050
and you're having
those conversations through an

00:41:05.050 --> 00:41:08.980
expression of needs on
both sides, a reciprocal

00:41:08.980 --> 00:41:11.170
expression of needs. Usually,

00:41:11.170 --> 00:41:14.560
that is the Black Swan that
will bust right through that blocker.

00:41:16.800 --> 00:41:21.570
- Amazing. Thank you so
much, Matt. That's incredibly helpful.

00:41:21.570 --> 00:41:24.870
Jumping back over to some
of the questions in the chat.

00:41:24.870 --> 00:41:27.120
Alan, I see you had a question

00:41:27.120 --> 00:41:29.940
around some of the
regulatory requirements.

00:41:29.940 --> 00:41:31.880
Do you want to jump in here?

00:41:31.880 --> 00:41:36.920
- Yeah, sure, thanks. This
is really, really interesting today.

00:41:36.920 --> 00:41:40.640
That kind of following on the
thread that you started with about

00:41:40.640 --> 00:41:44.000
these opportunities coming from
data to democratize this industry.

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:46.190
I wonder if there's
a counterpart

00:41:46.190 --> 00:41:50.570
regulatory piece that you see.
Are there regulatory gaps in

00:41:50.570 --> 00:41:53.330
that being able to be
successful in this industry

00:41:53.330 --> 00:41:56.630
that you describe as being
a monopolistic cabal?

00:41:56.630 --> 00:41:58.880
Are there regulatory things
that you think are really

00:41:58.880 --> 00:42:03.760
important either for
consumer protection or for

00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:06.470
pushing back against

00:42:06.470 --> 00:42:07.520
anticompetitive, kind of,

00:42:07.520 --> 00:42:10.130
practices that would
make a difference?

00:42:10.130 --> 00:42:13.070
- Yes. And this is where

00:42:13.070 --> 00:42:17.330
my opinions are probably
the most provocative.

00:42:17.330 --> 00:42:22.100
I believe that, by and large,

00:42:22.100 --> 00:42:26.420
insurance is massively
over-regulated, massively, to

00:42:26.420 --> 00:42:29.360
the point that it's actually
been regulated in such a way

00:42:29.360 --> 00:42:33.770
that it's extremely difficult for
new entrants to get in, right?

00:42:33.770 --> 00:42:36.650
I think we've democratized some of
it with a lot of the things

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:40.610
that companies like Valen
have done and other companies

00:42:40.610 --> 00:42:44.660
constructing consortium data sets.
But the regulatory world

00:42:44.660 --> 00:42:49.430
in insurance is extremely
antiquated. It is--

00:42:49.430 --> 00:42:54.710
they're basically operating on
1970s principle, by and large.

00:42:54.710 --> 00:42:58.460
And those principles were set
up by the insurance industry at

00:42:58.460 --> 00:43:03.830
that time, which was populated by
a very few, extremely large carriers,

00:43:03.830 --> 00:43:06.020
specifically for the purposes

00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:09.170
of creating barriers to
entry for new entrants.

00:43:09.170 --> 00:43:09.800
So,

00:43:09.800 --> 00:43:11.570
I believe all of
the regulations need to be

00:43:11.570 --> 00:43:12.560
completely rewritten,

00:43:12.560 --> 00:43:15.290
and they need to be completely
rewritten in view of the latest

00:43:15.290 --> 00:43:17.210
technologies.

00:43:17.210 --> 00:43:20.300
There is something that one
has to understand about insurance,

00:43:20.300 --> 00:43:22.490
and that is that

00:43:22.490 --> 00:43:26.750
I don't-- it is an
asymmetric information game.

00:43:26.750 --> 00:43:28.910
The insurance carrier
gets to ask questions.

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:32.210
And they have to ask
questions mostly through a third party,

00:43:32.210 --> 00:43:34.400
through the insurance
agent, right? And so,

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:37.700
not only do you have
information asymmetry, but you

00:43:37.700 --> 00:43:42.110
have information distortion
as well. And so, what the

00:43:42.110 --> 00:43:45.800
insurance companies are trying to
do is put themselves on a

00:43:45.800 --> 00:43:47.840
level playing field because
the insured has all of the

00:43:47.840 --> 00:43:51.650
knowledge about what their exposures
and what the risks are.

00:43:51.650 --> 00:43:55.220
The insurance agent has
probably 20% of that information,

00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:57.530
and the insurance carrier
probably has about 10% of that

00:43:57.530 --> 00:44:01.070
information. A lot of
what's coming available now

00:44:01.070 --> 00:44:04.460
is actually increasing that
10% to maybe 30% or 35% of the

00:44:04.460 --> 00:44:08.780
information. There's still
a conscientious disagreeable

00:44:08.780 --> 00:44:11.660
people out there that are
lying about all of their information.

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:12.620
They're a roofer,

00:44:12.620 --> 00:44:17.030
and they're saying that
they're a computer programming company.

00:44:17.030 --> 00:44:17.990
They're a trucker,

00:44:17.990 --> 00:44:21.050
and they're saying that
they're a retail shop, right?

00:44:21.050 --> 00:44:22.190
And that--

00:44:22.190 --> 00:44:26.150
that's where we're models
are actually quite good at

00:44:26.150 --> 00:44:29.720
determining whether someone is actually
lying to you or not,

00:44:29.720 --> 00:44:32.940
and identifying and shaving
down the moral hazard.

00:44:32.940 --> 00:44:35.180
Now, having said that,

00:44:35.180 --> 00:44:37.670
there are still ways now,

00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:39.560
and will be ways in the future,

00:44:39.560 --> 00:44:42.980
that insurance companies can
take advantage of information

00:44:42.980 --> 00:44:46.790
that the insurer doesn't
even know they have. And so,

00:44:46.790 --> 00:44:50.420
I think that information transparency is
a huge piece of the puzzle.

00:44:50.420 --> 00:44:54.230
I think that if an
insurance company is making a decision,

00:44:54.230 --> 00:44:56.030
letting the insurer
know what decision--

00:44:56.030 --> 00:44:58.160
what information they're using

00:44:58.160 --> 00:45:00.170
in order to render
that decision,

00:45:00.170 --> 00:45:03.740
is probably going to be very
important in the future.

00:45:03.740 --> 00:45:06.290
But I think that the
regulations around machine

00:45:06.290 --> 00:45:10.250
learning and filing models with
the Department of Insurance and

00:45:10.250 --> 00:45:14.270
the scrutiny that they have
to go through is massively, like,

00:45:14.270 --> 00:45:18.200
exponentially slowing down the
innovation that could occur in

00:45:18.200 --> 00:45:19.250
the insurance industry.

00:45:19.250 --> 00:45:22.190
It's also why most insurance
companies that I've worked with

00:45:22.190 --> 00:45:24.290
are choosing not to
file their models.

00:45:24.290 --> 00:45:26.810
There are very few people
that are actually filing models in

00:45:26.810 --> 00:45:28.100
the commercial insurance space.

00:45:28.100 --> 00:45:30.890
In the personal auto space
or the personal life space,

00:45:30.890 --> 00:45:32.600
you have to file
all of your models.

00:45:32.600 --> 00:45:34.700
So the Department of
Insurance gets to look at it,

00:45:34.700 --> 00:45:35.810
they get to scrutinize it,

00:45:35.810 --> 00:45:38.870
they can say no on just
about anything and they do.

00:45:38.870 --> 00:45:42.290
And there are some states that
don't even allow for trade secrets.

00:45:42.290 --> 00:45:45.020
So once you have to file
your model with the Department of Insurance,

00:45:45.020 --> 00:45:47.810
your competitor can actually get
all of that information and

00:45:47.810 --> 00:45:50.300
rebuild your model, which
I've done several times.

00:45:50.300 --> 00:45:53.450
So I think there needs
to be massive changes

00:45:53.450 --> 00:45:56.430
in regulation around
data science, machine learning,

00:45:56.430 --> 00:45:59.240
and algorithms, and
how they're used

00:45:59.240 --> 00:46:01.400
in determining pricing

00:46:01.400 --> 00:46:04.830
and availability of
insurance coverage.

00:46:04.830 --> 00:46:08.330
But I think it also needs to
be in light of the fact that

00:46:08.330 --> 00:46:11.420
insurance carriers are playing
an information asymmetric game.

00:46:11.420 --> 00:46:14.210
We do not have as much
information as we need in order

00:46:14.210 --> 00:46:16.700
to render the best
possible coverage and pricing decision

00:46:16.700 --> 00:46:18.890
on an individual
business. And really,

00:46:18.890 --> 00:46:19.950
that's what we're trying to do.

00:46:19.950 --> 00:46:21.770
We're essentially
buying used cars.

00:46:21.770 --> 00:46:24.230
And we have to figure out
whether that used car has had

00:46:24.230 --> 00:46:26.230
something bad that seller

00:46:28.000 --> 00:46:30.040
of that used car knows exactly
what's going on with that car.

00:46:30.040 --> 00:46:31.780
We don't know exactly
what that car.

00:46:31.780 --> 00:46:34.140
And that's what I think most
insurance carriers are trying to solve.

00:46:36.620 --> 00:46:37.330
- Thanks there's a bunch.

00:46:37.330 --> 00:46:39.710
that's super interesting
in a lot of perspective,

00:46:39.710 --> 00:46:42.640
especially about
asymmetric information.

00:46:42.640 --> 00:46:45.040
That's new for me to think
about. Appreciate hearing it. Thank you.

00:46:45.040 --> 00:46:47.540
- Absolutely.

00:46:47.540 --> 00:46:49.930
- AJ, I see you had a
question in the chat as well.

00:46:49.930 --> 00:46:51.930
Do you want to jump in here?

00:46:53.630 --> 00:46:59.060
- Sure, yeah. I wanted to get
your opinion on, and see how far

00:46:59.060 --> 00:47:01.160
some of these job
functions being automated,

00:47:01.160 --> 00:47:04.460
such as underwriting
or app input

00:47:04.460 --> 00:47:06.470
because AI is so much advanced,

00:47:06.470 --> 00:47:09.950
and it's reading so much
data from your database, vast

00:47:09.950 --> 00:47:11.770
databases, so

00:47:11.770 --> 00:47:13.290
what are your thoughts on that?

00:47:13.290 --> 00:47:16.320
- Yeah. So there's-- I
don't believe that all risks. And

00:47:16.320 --> 00:47:18.230
now, with

00:47:18.230 --> 00:47:20.690
the latest technology,

00:47:20.690 --> 00:47:23.000
my opinion is changing,

00:47:23.000 --> 00:47:23.270
sort of,

00:47:23.270 --> 00:47:25.820
actively as I start to
understand a little bit more

00:47:25.820 --> 00:47:27.600
about these LLMs.

00:47:27.600 --> 00:47:31.310
You know, in
Transformers and the like.

00:47:31.310 --> 00:47:33.880
I believe that probably

00:47:33.880 --> 00:47:35.890
85% to 90%,

00:47:35.890 --> 00:47:38.830
maybe even 95% of the
underwriting decisions that are

00:47:38.830 --> 00:47:41.920
currently being made by
human beings in the world,

00:47:41.920 --> 00:47:45.160
will be eliminated through the
use of machine learning and AI.

00:47:45.160 --> 00:47:48.230
I mean, that's
just a simple fact.

00:47:48.230 --> 00:47:51.700
It is already the case
that machine learning models can

00:47:51.700 --> 00:47:56.590
make significantly better and
more consistent decisions than

00:47:56.590 --> 00:47:57.190
humans.

00:47:57.190 --> 00:48:01.870
Now, it is game
theoretic, right? So what

00:48:01.870 --> 00:48:05.690
the models are not quite
as good yet at doing,

00:48:05.690 --> 00:48:09.070
and obviously, there's ways
to solve this using models.

00:48:09.070 --> 00:48:13.690
They're not quite as good
at understanding the market conditions,

00:48:13.690 --> 00:48:16.540
and the changing market
conditions with regard to what

00:48:16.540 --> 00:48:19.180
the best price might be, right?

00:48:19.180 --> 00:48:21.760
Insurance carriers have to be
able to understand good risk

00:48:21.760 --> 00:48:24.790
from bad risk, but they
also have to write policies, right?

00:48:24.790 --> 00:48:27.530
And that means that sometimes
you have to make trade-offs.

00:48:27.530 --> 00:48:28.960
And that's where
human beings come in.

00:48:28.960 --> 00:48:30.130
They're making
those trade-offs.

00:48:30.130 --> 00:48:30.940
They're saying, well,

00:48:30.940 --> 00:48:33.170
I might write this at a
slightly higher loss ratio.

00:48:33.170 --> 00:48:34.930
But if I don't write this

00:48:34.930 --> 00:48:38.050
packet of business, a
slightly higher loss ratio,

00:48:38.050 --> 00:48:39.970
I'm not going to have
enough revenue, right?

00:48:39.970 --> 00:48:43.870
So the game theoretic, piece
of the puzzle sort of price

00:48:43.870 --> 00:48:47.170
optimization, which is
a really bad word in

00:48:47.170 --> 00:48:49.600
insurance, but that's
how we think, right?

00:48:49.600 --> 00:48:52.010
We're actually doing price
optimization every single day.

00:48:52.010 --> 00:48:54.310
It's just that we're
doing it through human underwriters.

00:48:54.310 --> 00:48:57.610
I think where the world is
going to go in insurance is

00:48:57.610 --> 00:49:00.910
that a lot of the customer
service personnel are going to

00:49:00.910 --> 00:49:05.980
be eliminated because LLMs are going
to take on that. You know, Chatbots

00:49:05.980 --> 00:49:09.280
and RPA and automation are
going to be able to do probably

00:49:09.280 --> 00:49:15.110
95% to 100% of policy
changes endorsements, things like that.

00:49:15.110 --> 00:49:16.720
But on the underwriting side,

00:49:16.720 --> 00:49:20.260
I think that it will probably
eliminate 85% to 90% of the

00:49:20.260 --> 00:49:24.070
decisions that doesn't mean
that the underwriter's role

00:49:24.070 --> 00:49:24.940
gets eliminated.

00:49:24.940 --> 00:49:27.820
It means that the underwriter's
role changes significantly.

00:49:27.820 --> 00:49:31.570
It changes to a portfolio
management role. It changes to

00:49:31.570 --> 00:49:33.790
a gap identification role.

00:49:33.790 --> 00:49:37.180
And it changes to a role where
an underwriter is now the

00:49:37.180 --> 00:49:40.390
subject matter expert that
works directly with the data

00:49:40.390 --> 00:49:45.670
science team in order to
identify and explore the gaps

00:49:45.670 --> 00:49:47.830
that may exist in
the model solutions,

00:49:47.830 --> 00:49:51.130
and helps to fill
those with novel data

00:49:51.130 --> 00:49:54.880
and perspective that sometimes a
lot of the data science teams

00:49:54.880 --> 00:49:57.580
don't have because they're
not on the front lines.

00:49:57.580 --> 00:49:59.770
And that's sort of
a surrogate is--

00:49:59.770 --> 00:50:02.470
it is really important in
insurance that the data science

00:50:02.470 --> 00:50:04.600
team actually has a working
knowledge of the line of

00:50:04.600 --> 00:50:06.340
business that they're actually

00:50:06.340 --> 00:50:09.280
looking at. So if you are
building a model for commercial

00:50:09.280 --> 00:50:11.080
auto, you have to
understand commercial auto.

00:50:11.080 --> 00:50:12.130
You have to understand
how it's rated,

00:50:12.130 --> 00:50:14.410
you have to understand
the coverages, et cetera.

00:50:14.410 --> 00:50:17.290
I have a lot of PhD data
scientists that I've worked

00:50:17.290 --> 00:50:19.450
with in the past that
felt like, hey, I'm a data

00:50:19.450 --> 00:50:23.320
scientist. I don't need to know
the underlying environment.

00:50:23.320 --> 00:50:24.730
I'm just going
to grab the data,

00:50:24.730 --> 00:50:26.380
and I'm going to build a model.

00:50:26.380 --> 00:50:29.980
I can tell you, that
in my experience, 90%

00:50:29.980 --> 00:50:33.130
of the models that are built
in that fashion fail because they

00:50:33.130 --> 00:50:34.480
cannot be productionalized.

00:50:34.480 --> 00:50:38.020
The data scientists didn't find
out whether the information was

00:50:38.020 --> 00:50:41.410
available, at the time that
the decision needed to be made.

00:50:41.410 --> 00:50:44.380
Or the target was not
transformed in a way that was

00:50:44.380 --> 00:50:47.560
critically important because the
data was centered, censored,

00:50:47.560 --> 00:50:50.020
or there was a treatment effect
that they did not identify

00:50:50.020 --> 00:50:51.490
until after they built them up.

00:50:51.490 --> 00:50:53.490
And they have to rebuild
the whole model over again.

00:50:57.150 --> 00:50:59.280
- Thank you. That-- this

00:50:59.280 --> 00:51:02.460
is kind of a follow-up
question here but talking about building

00:51:02.460 --> 00:51:05.910
this model. There was
an anonymous question.

00:51:05.910 --> 00:51:06.180
It was,

00:51:06.180 --> 00:51:08.970
can you walk through how your
team approaches finding a

00:51:08.970 --> 00:51:12.420
valuable question,
exploring that question,

00:51:12.420 --> 00:51:14.460
building and testing
a predictive model,

00:51:14.460 --> 00:51:19.640
and then deploying that? The
technical aspect of that?

00:51:19.640 --> 00:51:21.260
- I'm going to try
and be concise there.

00:51:21.260 --> 00:51:23.150
I'm not sure how
concise I can be.

00:51:23.150 --> 00:51:23.540
- That's OK.

00:51:23.540 --> 00:51:25.010
- So,

00:51:25.010 --> 00:51:28.250
generally speaking, we will,

00:51:28.250 --> 00:51:30.710
on a regular basis, go out
to each one of the Department

00:51:30.710 --> 00:51:34.160
heads. And we will
ask them, what

00:51:34.160 --> 00:51:38.060
are your problems today? What
is creating too much friction?

00:51:38.060 --> 00:51:40.880
What is something
that you think

00:51:40.880 --> 00:51:44.240
could be better decision
that's being partly decision today?

00:51:44.240 --> 00:51:47.300
And then we'll get all
of that information back.

00:51:47.300 --> 00:51:50.750
Once we construct all of the
quote-unquote, "potential

00:51:50.750 --> 00:51:53.510
opportunities"
for data science,

00:51:53.510 --> 00:51:57.440
then we start to look around at
the available data sources that we have,

00:51:57.440 --> 00:52:00.620
and we start to do some
discovery around whether or not

00:52:00.620 --> 00:52:02.930
we can actually solve that
particular problem with data.

00:52:02.930 --> 00:52:06.290
Do we have the data available
to be able to solve that problem?

00:52:06.290 --> 00:52:09.410
Is that problem a big
enough problem to solve?

00:52:09.410 --> 00:52:13.160
So, usually, once we go
to the Department heads, we'll then,

00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:16.010
go to the business or
department analysts within that

00:52:16.010 --> 00:52:17.820
particular department
and say, hey,

00:52:17.820 --> 00:52:20.690
how big of a problem is
this? Can we quantify

00:52:20.690 --> 00:52:23.450
the friction? Can we quantify
how much this is costing us?

00:52:23.450 --> 00:52:27.110
How many minutes? How many
hours? How many days? How

00:52:27.110 --> 00:52:29.390
many employee

00:52:29.390 --> 00:52:31.010
days does this take?

00:52:31.010 --> 00:52:32.900
And what, theoretically,

00:52:32.900 --> 00:52:35.660
if we were able to remove
that and automate that,

00:52:35.660 --> 00:52:38.330
what would the savings
be? And then, of course,

00:52:38.330 --> 00:52:39.890
there's other
things in insurance.

00:52:39.890 --> 00:52:43.620
How much better can we make
around these decisions?

00:52:43.620 --> 00:52:46.550
Whether it be, can I separate
good risk from bad risk?

00:52:46.550 --> 00:52:48.980
Can I identify the
probability that someone's actually going

00:52:48.980 --> 00:52:50.090
to pay their bills?

00:52:50.090 --> 00:52:53.240
Can I identify the cost if
they don't pay their bills?

00:52:53.240 --> 00:52:55.700
Can I identify how many times
they're not going to pay their bills?

00:52:55.700 --> 00:52:57.780
And we've got to do
something with that policy,

00:52:57.780 --> 00:52:59.300
whether it be to cancel
it, and reinstate it,

00:52:59.300 --> 00:53:02.450
and all that stuff because
that cost insurance company money.

00:53:02.450 --> 00:53:05.720
All of those different things,
even on the marketing side. So,

00:53:05.720 --> 00:53:09.800
what is our what's our current
customer acquisition cost?

00:53:09.800 --> 00:53:12.600
And where is it
relatively high?

00:53:12.600 --> 00:53:15.290
And is there a way for us to
build a model in order to lower that?

00:53:15.290 --> 00:53:19.460
So there's a wide variety of
different things that you can do. Once

00:53:19.460 --> 00:53:20.690
you sit on a problem,

00:53:20.690 --> 00:53:22.160
and you know that there's
enough value there,

00:53:22.160 --> 00:53:25.220
and you know that you have the
data to be able to solve that

00:53:25.220 --> 00:53:27.590
problem, then it's

00:53:27.590 --> 00:53:30.080
a pretty standard process.
You have to wrangle the data,

00:53:30.080 --> 00:53:31.220
you have to normalize the data,

00:53:31.220 --> 00:53:34.760
you have to work together
with the Department experts in that

00:53:34.760 --> 00:53:38.660
department to identify what
they think might be interesting

00:53:38.660 --> 00:53:41.060
or valuable covariates, right?

00:53:41.060 --> 00:53:41.780
So, usually,

00:53:41.780 --> 00:53:45.230
we will do some unsupervised
modeling just to figure out

00:53:45.230 --> 00:53:46.550
what the data
looks like, right?

00:53:46.550 --> 00:53:50.280
Where might there be blooms
of data that could be interesting?

00:53:50.280 --> 00:53:52.440
And then we'll do a
correlation study,

00:53:52.440 --> 00:53:55.040
which is with as many
covariates that we can actually

00:53:55.040 --> 00:53:57.860
put together, and then bring
that back to the Department.

00:53:57.860 --> 00:54:00.230
And then ask the
subject matter expert,

00:54:00.230 --> 00:54:03.290
what's not in here that you
think should be in here? Right?

00:54:03.290 --> 00:54:03.770
And then,

00:54:03.770 --> 00:54:06.500
they'll start to think
about and construct some additional

00:54:06.500 --> 00:54:09.320
features, then you start
the feature engineering phase, right?

00:54:09.320 --> 00:54:12.860
You do that with the
subject matter experts in that department,

00:54:12.860 --> 00:54:15.050
and you try and get as
many features that you think might

00:54:15.050 --> 00:54:17.360
be useful as possible.

00:54:17.360 --> 00:54:18.180
And then, of course,

00:54:18.180 --> 00:54:20.210
there's some target
transformation sometimes.

00:54:20.210 --> 00:54:21.950
Are you going to build
a logistic model?

00:54:21.950 --> 00:54:23.900
Are you going to
build a linear model?

00:54:23.900 --> 00:54:26.870
Are you going to build something
that you're going to use for triage?

00:54:26.870 --> 00:54:29.870
Is it going to be sort
of a categorical model where you've

00:54:29.870 --> 00:54:31.580
got five or six different

00:54:31.580 --> 00:54:33.140
decisions that need to be made,

00:54:33.140 --> 00:54:34.640
and you're going
to split those up?

00:54:34.640 --> 00:54:38.930
You kind of figure out what
the best sort of opportunity is there.

00:54:38.930 --> 00:54:41.720
And then, we usually go through
sample and partitioning.

00:54:41.720 --> 00:54:42.830
How are you going
to sample this?

00:54:42.830 --> 00:54:44.960
What's your strategy
going to be?

00:54:44.960 --> 00:54:46.680
What is your
validation strategy?

00:54:46.680 --> 00:54:48.770
So are you going
to do cross-fold validation?

00:54:48.770 --> 00:54:50.190
What's your holdout
going to look like?

00:54:50.190 --> 00:54:51.590
What's your train set
going to look like?

00:54:51.590 --> 00:54:53.460
What you're testing is
that going to look like?

00:54:53.460 --> 00:54:57.560
Are those stratified correctly?
Is anything time series based?

00:54:57.560 --> 00:55:01.400
Because that has a significant
impact on how you're thinking

00:55:01.400 --> 00:55:03.350
about your sampling strategy.

00:55:03.350 --> 00:55:07.670
Usually, in insurance,
depending on data size

00:55:07.670 --> 00:55:12.080
and the composition of the
target, we use anywhere between

00:55:12.080 --> 00:55:16.310
three partitions and 10
partitions, right? If it's a

00:55:16.310 --> 00:55:18.590
more complicated problem
and we've got a lot of data,

00:55:18.590 --> 00:55:19.880
we use up to 10 partitions,

00:55:19.880 --> 00:55:21.980
and we'll hold three or
four of them out. And then,

00:55:21.980 --> 00:55:26.360
we'll use incrementally, four or five
or six or seven of those

00:55:26.360 --> 00:55:29.870
in order to increment
through the overall model-building process.

00:55:29.870 --> 00:55:33.740
We'll use the first
partition, maybe split that up

00:55:33.740 --> 00:55:36.680
in order to do
feature engineering,

00:55:36.680 --> 00:55:39.680
and basic variable
selection and reduction,

00:55:39.680 --> 00:55:42.260
then we will build
our initial model,

00:55:42.260 --> 00:55:44.840
and then we'll test it on
the next partition. And then,

00:55:44.840 --> 00:55:47.060
normally, what we do
is, we'll build on--

00:55:47.060 --> 00:55:49.040
let's say you
have five different partitions.

00:55:49.040 --> 00:55:50.720
We build on one and
two tests on three,

00:55:50.720 --> 00:55:52.130
build on one and
three tests on two.

00:55:52.130 --> 00:55:54.170
Build on two and three
tests on one, right?

00:55:54.170 --> 00:55:56.180
And then once you're really
confident that you've got the

00:55:56.180 --> 00:55:59.240
right model using that data,
you add the next partition.

00:55:59.240 --> 00:56:01.760
Now, you've got one two,
and three built on four. Four,

00:56:01.760 --> 00:56:03.530
three, and two, built on--

00:56:03.530 --> 00:56:06.500
tested on one, and so
on and so forth, right?

00:56:06.500 --> 00:56:08.930
And we're constantly
looking at coefficients,

00:56:08.930 --> 00:56:10.430
whether they're shifting,
whether they're going up and

00:56:10.430 --> 00:56:12.170
down. If the tree model,

00:56:12.170 --> 00:56:14.690
we're looking at what
the splits look like.

00:56:14.690 --> 00:56:17.060
We're sort of doing
hyperparameter selection

00:56:17.060 --> 00:56:21.140
usually on those early partitions until
you get to the final model,

00:56:21.140 --> 00:56:24.560
usually with two or three
validations that's already held out.

00:56:24.560 --> 00:56:25.850
And in insurance,

00:56:25.850 --> 00:56:28.370
we're usually using between
five and eight cross-fold

00:56:28.370 --> 00:56:33.830
validation at each one of those stages
just to make sure that we're getting--

00:56:33.830 --> 00:56:37.160
there's a lot of volatility because
of the zero point max in insurance.

00:56:37.160 --> 00:56:38.960
So you really need to make
sure that you've got enough

00:56:38.960 --> 00:56:42.740
cross-fold validation there because you
can have a very large loss

00:56:42.740 --> 00:56:45.800
that makes things look really
weird in any one partition of

00:56:45.800 --> 00:56:47.800
the data that
you're looking at.

00:56:47.800 --> 00:56:49.180
Hopefully, that helps.

00:56:49.180 --> 00:56:51.820
Because I can go on that
for like an hour and a half.

00:56:51.820 --> 00:56:52.940
- No, thank you very much.

00:56:52.940 --> 00:56:55.970
We threw a lot at you
with that one question.

00:56:55.970 --> 00:56:58.570
I think we have time
for one more question.

00:56:58.570 --> 00:57:00.820
[? Callau, ?] I
see you had asked

00:57:00.820 --> 00:57:02.870
something a bit
earlier about training.

00:57:02.870 --> 00:57:04.870
Do you want to jump
in with your question?

00:57:06.690 --> 00:57:09.840
- Yeah, sure. I'm happy to
jump in. Hi, Matt. My name is

00:57:09.840 --> 00:57:14.240
[? Callau. ?] I'm a
customer success rep here at Posit.

00:57:14.240 --> 00:57:16.760
- She's actually my
customer success.

00:57:16.760 --> 00:57:19.430
- Yes, for Pie Insurance.

00:57:19.430 --> 00:57:22.140
And a few others on the
call, actually. But, yeah,

00:57:22.140 --> 00:57:24.710
there seems to be so much
to know in this space,

00:57:24.710 --> 00:57:28.940
not just for insurance, but
data science programming, in general.

00:57:28.940 --> 00:57:32.300
And as somebody who might
be just starting out in industry,

00:57:32.300 --> 00:57:34.880
or coming out of college,

00:57:34.880 --> 00:57:37.970
how would they get
a job? I guess,

00:57:37.970 --> 00:57:42.950
what skills or knowledge do you
think they should have? Or what

00:57:42.950 --> 00:57:46.790
are teams like yours
looking for in new hires?

00:57:46.790 --> 00:57:47.330
Like, do you--

00:57:47.330 --> 00:57:48.080
- That's a really
good question.

00:57:48.080 --> 00:57:48.380
- Yeah.

00:57:48.380 --> 00:57:49.520
- I mean,

00:57:49.520 --> 00:57:51.980
so, I think a working
knowledge of both Python,

00:57:51.980 --> 00:57:55.910
and I'm going to say it,
R, right? R and Python, and a

00:57:55.910 --> 00:57:59.180
working knowledge of both of
those is really important. The

00:57:59.180 --> 00:58:04.610
biggest piece of the puzzle
for me is an understanding of

00:58:04.610 --> 00:58:06.860
what I would call pragmatic

00:58:06.860 --> 00:58:10.610
productionalizable
model building, right?

00:58:10.610 --> 00:58:12.320
Do not

00:58:12.320 --> 00:58:14.780
narrow your scope

00:58:14.780 --> 00:58:17.160
or your interest down
to research alone.

00:58:17.160 --> 00:58:20.240
You need to know how to
get models into production so that

00:58:20.240 --> 00:58:23.840
they can be used by actors
in the real world, right?

00:58:23.840 --> 00:58:27.200
If you're doing
descriptive statistics,

00:58:27.200 --> 00:58:29.840
that's great. I mean-- and if
that's what you want to do, great.

00:58:29.840 --> 00:58:32.990
But you're going to cut
yourself short if you don't

00:58:32.990 --> 00:58:34.100
know how to deploy a model.

00:58:34.100 --> 00:58:36.440
You're going to cut yourself
short if you don't know what

00:58:36.440 --> 00:58:39.200
the process look like in
order to deploy a model.

00:58:39.200 --> 00:58:41.300
You don't necessarily need to
know how to do the engineering,

00:58:41.300 --> 00:58:43.460
right? You can get an
MLOps team for that.

00:58:43.460 --> 00:58:45.260
But you need to know,
as a data scientist,

00:58:45.260 --> 00:58:47.960
how to do things
like parity testing,

00:58:47.960 --> 00:58:50.210
predictions between the
production environment and the

00:58:50.210 --> 00:58:51.650
model build environment, right?

00:58:51.650 --> 00:58:53.150
You need to understand
what that looks like,

00:58:53.150 --> 00:58:54.710
with that process
looks like, right?

00:58:54.710 --> 00:58:58.370
And you need to understand how
to actually go the last mile,

00:58:58.370 --> 00:58:59.600
if you will, right?

00:58:59.600 --> 00:59:01.910
If you're just building general
linear models and you're doing

00:59:01.910 --> 00:59:03.380
that descriptively, and you're
saying, look, I built the

00:59:03.380 --> 00:59:04.640
model, and it
looks great, right?

00:59:04.640 --> 00:59:07.610
The next question that any
executive is going to ask you

00:59:07.610 --> 00:59:10.610
is, OK, how do we use this?
How are we going to deploy this?

00:59:10.610 --> 00:59:11.930
Are we going to
deploy it in SQL?

00:59:11.930 --> 00:59:14.370
Are we going to containerize it?
Are we going to use Scala?

00:59:14.370 --> 00:59:18.440
Are we going to do something else
in order to get that model into PMML,

00:59:18.440 --> 00:59:19.460
whatever the case may be?

00:59:19.460 --> 00:59:21.710
How do we get that
model into production?

00:59:21.710 --> 00:59:24.170
And how do we know that
production model is going to

00:59:24.170 --> 00:59:27.230
score the same way as it
did in the model build environment?

00:59:27.230 --> 00:59:28.520
I think the other one is,

00:59:28.520 --> 00:59:31.280
having a really good
understanding and an ability to

00:59:31.280 --> 00:59:34.460
articulate what the model
building process looks like.

00:59:34.460 --> 00:59:37.820
All the way from finding
the data sources,

00:59:37.820 --> 00:59:40.610
all the way through to
validating that model and

00:59:40.610 --> 00:59:43.640
making sure that you
don't have an over model,

00:59:43.640 --> 00:59:46.670
and that model is really going
to perform as you say it's

00:59:46.670 --> 00:59:49.850
going to perform in the real
world on data that it hasn't

00:59:49.850 --> 00:59:51.800
seen before. Those are
all really, really important.

00:59:51.800 --> 00:59:53.210
The last point
that I'll make is,

00:59:53.210 --> 00:59:59.060
it is critically important for you to
show in any interview or to any

00:59:59.060 --> 01:00:03.500
business manager that you have
a high level of curiosity in

01:00:03.500 --> 01:00:06.650
the business that
you're in. Don't

01:00:06.650 --> 01:00:10.050
sell yourself short, and
just be a data scientist.

01:00:10.050 --> 01:00:12.800
You need to be a curious
data scientist. If it's an

01:00:12.800 --> 01:00:16.220
insurance, you need to want
to learn insurance. If it's in

01:00:16.220 --> 01:00:19.040
Aeronautics, you need to
want to learn Aeronautics.

01:00:19.040 --> 01:00:22.220
If it's in process optimization
for-- I don't know,

01:00:22.220 --> 01:00:23.450
making candy bars,

01:00:23.450 --> 01:00:26.240
you need to really be curious
and interested in what the

01:00:26.240 --> 01:00:28.910
process looks like to
make a candy bar, right?

01:00:28.910 --> 01:00:30.390
I think that that's really,

01:00:30.390 --> 01:00:33.170
really important is that data
scientists can't just be data

01:00:33.170 --> 01:00:37.250
scientists. They have to
be Renaissance people,

01:00:37.250 --> 01:00:40.460
and constantly curious,
and autodidactic.

01:00:40.460 --> 01:00:42.980
That-- that's the best advice

01:00:42.980 --> 01:00:44.720
that I can give

01:00:44.720 --> 01:00:48.710
is, don't narrow
yourself. Don't be

01:00:48.710 --> 01:00:51.560
an inch wide and a mile deep.

01:00:51.560 --> 01:00:53.660
Be a mile wide and

01:00:53.660 --> 01:00:56.730
more than a few inches deep.

01:00:56.730 --> 01:00:57.880
- Great. Thanks, Matt.

01:00:57.880 --> 01:00:59.060
- Yep.

01:00:59.060 --> 01:01:00.080
- Thank you so much, Matt.

01:01:00.080 --> 01:01:02.820
I know we quickly came to
the top of the hour here.

01:01:02.820 --> 01:01:05.690
But I really appreciated
this conversation,

01:01:05.690 --> 01:01:08.430
and you jumping on
here and joining us.

01:01:08.430 --> 01:01:10.760
- Absolutely my pleasure.
I had a fun time.

01:01:10.760 --> 01:01:12.960
Thank you all for all
the great questions too.

01:01:12.960 --> 01:01:15.860
I'm going to put
Matt's LinkedIn in the

01:01:15.860 --> 01:01:18.560
chat here if anybody wants to

01:01:18.560 --> 01:01:19.640
connect. But also,

01:01:19.640 --> 01:01:22.910
I will share the recording of
course to YouTube and to the

01:01:22.910 --> 01:01:25.670
